  "enhanced_information": "The clinical presentation of a 3-year-old male with sore throat, fever, and dark inflammatory exudate, along with the culture results from cysteine-tellurite agar producing black, iridescent colonies, is indicative of Corynebacterium diphtheriae, the causative agent of diphtheria. This organism is characterized by the presence of metachromic granules, which are storage granules that stain differently from the rest of the cell. Other options such as serpentine growth patterns, lancet-shaped morphology, long branching filaments, and kidney-bean shaped rods do not align with the known characteristics of Corynebacterium diphtheriae. Therefore, the most likely microscopic feature of the causal organism is the presence of metachromic granules."
